        date: 'July 1518 — “anno a Christo nato MDXVIII men. Iulio” in the Library of Congress transcription of the colophon, “anno MDXVIII mense Iulio” in the Folger’s; printed after Trithemius’s death in 1516',
        year: 1518,
        place: 'Basel or Oppenheim: the catalogues disagree. The colophon names the bookseller Johannes Haselberg “de Aia”, of the diocese of Constance, at whose cost the book was printed. Michael Furter and Adam Petri of Basel and Jakob Köbel of Oppenheim are each named as its printer; the Folger, which gives the printing chiefly to Furter, also records that he died between 5 March and 2 May 1517, more than a year before the colophon.',
        locator: 'The catalogue of alphabets, among them the Theban, in Book VI, which opens on sig. 2p5r; the Theban and the alchemist’s alphabet share sig. 2q5v. The 1518 printing is unpaginated: Joseph H. Peterson cites “Polygraphia 6, 1508, p. 579”, a page number that fits a later paginated edition, not the 1518. A bookseller’s description is reported to place the alphabets in Book V, as a Wikipedia talk page does for the French translation, which sets this matter under the running head of its fifth book, with the Theban at fol. 184v.',
        notes: 'The first printed witness to a Trithemian cipher alphabet that the sources report. It may not be the first witness. Whether the manuscript version Trithemius presented to Maximilian in 1508, edited by Maximilian Gamer (Brill, 2022), already holds the catalogue of alphabets is not known; and the Steganographia, begun in 1499, circulated in manuscript for a century before it was printed.',
